WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.700
Talk to your computer and watch it build a working app in minutes. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 234. How a Chatbot keeps a conversation straight, then a shared workspace for group projects, then QuickBits. Gemini Neon, described in Post on X, is a new feature in the Gemini app that builds an app from a spoken description. You say what you want out loud, the artificial intelligence or ai puts a real app on your screen with buttons that do real things. One request people are using is,

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.980
make me an app that follows homework due dates and fires reminders.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:38.400
It is close to describing a Minecraft house to someone who then places every block so the doors

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.960
actually open. The AI does not get tired and it can make the app save your data or hook it up

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:50.160
to other tools. For a science fair or group presentation, you spend time on the idea, not hours learning

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.860
to code a simple tool. Building your own study planner or a rough game becomes much faster to test.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:00.880
A lot of jobs will soon mean saying what you want an AI to make, rather than writing

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:01.860
every line yourself.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.920
Learning to explain your ideas clearly is the skill that counts most.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.560
Open the Gemini app on your phone or laptop and start talking with your voice.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:15.620
Say build me a task list that lines up jobs by due date and watch what it builds.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.760
You may need a free Google account to start and you do not need any coding.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.660
Get more specific and you can ask for an app that uses the weather to suggest things to

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:29.020
do outside, or one that sorts school trip photos into a short slide show.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.400
Starting from your voice, even someone who has never written code can test whether

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.060
an app idea is worth pursuing.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:40.260
Describe a problem you want solved, and the AI takes steps that once took weeks of study.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.180
You still need to understand what the app actually does.

00:01:44.252 --> 00:01:48.892
A chatbot, the AI you message back and forth, does not remember like a person.

00:01:49.532 --> 00:01:53.492
Analytics Insight explains, threads can run for hundreds of messages.

00:01:53.492 --> 00:01:58.812
images. Take a pop quiz that reprints every earlier answer, then adds one new question at

00:01:58.812 --> 00:02:03.652
the bottom. You send a new line, and in the background the app copies the full old thread

00:02:03.652 --> 00:02:09.352
and stacks your new words on top. That rebuilt packet goes to the model. The AI that writes

00:02:09.352 --> 00:02:14.852
the reply every single time. The model is trained to find patterns in large amounts of text,

00:02:15.232 --> 00:02:20.032
so it can grab the useful bits without getting lost. If the packet gets too thick to handle

00:02:20.032 --> 00:02:25.032
in one pass, the app may peel off the oldest pages or replace them with a short recap.

00:02:25.572 --> 00:02:29.692
That is why some chats slow down or forget older details after they run long.

00:02:30.332 --> 00:02:35.292
When a reply uses something from 10 messages ago, the model just took that reprinted test

00:02:35.292 --> 00:02:35.672
again.

00:02:36.252 --> 00:02:40.352
It keeps sending the full history in so the AI has what you would have after reading the

00:02:40.352 --> 00:02:41.212
chat from the start.

00:02:41.832 --> 00:02:43.352
The whole trick is that reprint.

00:02:43.872 --> 00:02:46.952
Once you see it, the long memory stops feeling like a superpower.

00:02:46.952 --> 00:02:53.226
Open AI as end gadget reports, open shared workspaces called spaces to regular users.

00:02:53.746 --> 00:02:58.706
You drop in documents, notes or files, then invite classmates or friends to work in the

00:02:58.706 --> 00:02:59.246
same chat.

00:02:59.826 --> 00:03:03.846
Everyone sees the same AI helper and can toss in ideas at the same time.

00:03:04.386 --> 00:03:08.546
Treat it like a shared class folder that can also answer questions about whatever you put

00:03:08.546 --> 00:03:08.926
inside.

00:03:09.626 --> 00:03:10.966
Try this as a mini challenge.

00:03:11.646 --> 00:03:16.766
Go to the chat GPT website, look for spaces in the sidebar, and create a new one.

00:03:16.766 --> 00:03:21.906
Upload a homework sheet or a store you are writing, invite someone to join and ask the

00:03:21.906 --> 00:03:23.146
AI questions together.

00:03:23.686 --> 00:03:28.606
Notice how every file and every AI reply stays in one place, so nobody hunts through

00:03:28.606 --> 00:03:30.486
separate chats or forwarded messages.

00:03:31.106 --> 00:03:35.366
When someone adds a new file, the AI can use it right away to answer the whole group.

00:03:35.806 --> 00:03:40.406
That helps when teammates live in different time zones, or when a writing group wants to brainstorm

00:03:40.406 --> 00:03:42.146
without losing earlier ideas.

00:03:42.146 --> 00:03:47.286
is. Everyone gets answers based on the same documents, so one person does not get different

00:03:47.286 --> 00:03:52.546
suggestions than another. The feature is aimed at plus users, which is a paid plan, so

00:03:52.546 --> 00:03:55.306
you may need a parent's help if you are under the sign-up age.

00:03:55.826 --> 00:04:01.186
Once you are inside, the steps stay simple. Google's search homepage now shows your past

00:04:01.186 --> 00:04:06.966
AI chats on the side, ours, technical notes, and the old search button is gone. You can see

00:04:06.966 --> 00:04:11.566
earlier conversations while you search the web as usual, so jumping between search and

00:04:11.566 --> 00:04:16.826
and AI chat feel smooth. Switching is closer to flipping tabs than opening a blank page.

00:04:17.326 --> 00:04:21.966
And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool. Go play

00:04:21.966 --> 00:04:27.766
